6. WHAT THE SOFTWARE DOES ON YOUR MACHINE

Stated plainly so that "at your own risk" is an informed choice, not a surprise:

  - It writes one folder, `%APPDATA%\Pharos\pharos`, holding the saved pane layout, the window
    position, a small diagnostics log, and a handshake file for the local control endpoint.
  - It starts the shell you choose — Git Bash, PowerShell or Command Prompt — as an ordinary child
    process, running as you, with your privileges and no others.
  - It reads the files and git status of the directories your panes are standing in, in order to
    display them. It never writes to them.
  - It creates no service, scheduled task or registry entry, changes no PATH, and installs nothing.
  - It contacts no network service and sends no telemetry. It listens on one socket, bound to
    127.0.0.1 only, on an OS-chosen port behind a per-launch token, so that local tooling can inspect
    the running application. `--no-control` disables it entirely.


